export const ELEMENT_DESCRIPTIONS: Record<string, string> = {
  Wood: `Wood element adds growth, creativity, and idealism to your Chinese zodiac sign. Wood people are expansive, flexible, and always reaching toward something new. They bend rather than break under pressure and possess a natural generosity that wants to see everyone around them flourish. Wood's shadow is overextension... growing in too many directions at once.`,
  Fire: `Fire element adds passion, enthusiasm, and dynamic energy to your Chinese zodiac sign. Fire people are bold, charismatic, and impossible to ignore... they light up whatever they touch and inspire action in others. They lead with confidence and live with intensity. Fire's shadow is burnout... the flame that burns brightest can also consume itself.`,
  Earth: `Earth element adds stability, practicality, and groundedness to your Chinese zodiac sign. Earth people build on solid foundations and possess a patience that others find deeply reassuring. They're the ones who turn vision into reality through steady, methodical effort. Earth's shadow is rigidity... the same stability that provides security can also resist necessary change.`,
  Metal: `Metal element adds determination, precision, and moral clarity to your Chinese zodiac sign. Metal people cut through confusion with sharp thinking and hold themselves to standards that others find impressive... or intimidating. They value discipline, honor, and the beauty of things done right. Metal's shadow is inflexibility... the same principles that guide them can also isolate them.`,
  Water: `Water element adds intuition, adaptability, and emotional depth to your Chinese zodiac sign. Water people flow around obstacles rather than fighting them and possess a perceptiveness that borders on the psychic. They understand the currents beneath the surface of any situation. Water's shadow is passivity... the same adaptability that navigates complexity can also avoid necessary confrontation.`,
}

// Combined animal x element interpretations (the 60-cell sexagenary matrix).
// Keyed `${element} ${animal}`. Each cell describes how the element inflects that
// animal's signature trait... whether it reads as gift or shadow. Filled
// progressively; getAnimalElement() falls back to the generic element text for
// any cell not yet written.
export const ANIMAL_ELEMENT: Record<string, string> = {
  'Wood Rat': `Wood is the element that wants to grow something, and it hands the Rat's cleverness a project bigger than the Rat itself. The result is the most generous version of the calculating mind ... resourcefulness pointed outward, the schemer who turns out to be building a garden. Wood Rats read a room the way every Rat does, but they tend to use what they find to lift the people in it, not just themselves. That is what gets them trusted. The opportunism that looks like self interest on a colder Rat looks, here, like vision ... the friend who always knows someone, always sees the angle, and keeps cutting you in.\n\nThe shadow is in the growing. Wood overextends. It reaches in too many directions at once, and the Wood Rat's quick eye for opportunity becomes a quick eye for too many opportunities ... each plan clever, none getting the patient finish that would have made it real, the generosity starting to feel like someone promising more rooms than the house has. The trust holds as long as the growth stays rooted. When it stops being rooted, the Wood Rat is just scattering seed and calling it a forest.`,
  'Fire Rat': `Fire takes the Rat's cleverness and puts it on stage. Where most Rats prefer to work the angles quietly, the Fire Rat performs them ... loud, magnetic, impossible to ignore, selling the plan with so much conviction that the room buys in before it has checked the math. This is the Rat as showman, and the charisma is not a trick. People trust the Fire Rat because the energy is real and the boldness is contagious.\n\nThen the heat reveals it. Fire lights up whatever it touches, including the working parts the Rat would rather keep dark. When a quieter Rat schemes, you never see the gears. When a Fire Rat schemes, the gears are lit from the inside, and the same charm that sold the room becomes evidence of how hard it was selling. That is the moment the Fire Rat turns from trusted to watched, and it happens fast. The flame that burns brightest consumes itself ... this Rat calculates at full intensity until it runs out of fuel in public. The version that lasts learns the difference between lighting the room and setting it on fire to prove that it can.`,
  'Earth Rat': `Earth slows everything down, and on the Rat it does something almost unfair ... it turns the quickest mind in the zodiac into the patient one. The Earth Rat still sees every opportunity the other Rats see. It just declines to pounce. It waits, weighs, builds, and lets timing do the work the other Rats try to force. This is the combination where the Rat's old association with wealth and surplus actually lands, because Earth understands that real surplus is built slowly and then kept. Of all the Rats, this is the one whose calculation reads cleanest as wisdom. You trust the Earth Rat because it has never once seemed to be in a hurry to take anything from you.\n\nThe shadow is the price of all that ground. Earth hardens ... the same steadiness that makes the Earth Rat dependable can set, over the years, into a refusal to move. The Rat's defining gift is adaptability. Earth can freeze it, the Earth Rat running the proven play long after the board has changed, mistaking patience for strategy when it has quietly become stubbornness. The thing to watch is the day it stops adapting and starts simply digging in ... because the one creature nobody expects to get stuck is the Rat.`,
  'Metal Rat': `Metal sharpens. It sharpens the Rat into the coldest, most exact version of itself ... the resourcefulness disciplined into precision, the opportunism organized into strategy, the whole clever apparatus run to a standard most people find either impressive or a little frightening. The Metal Rat does not improvise its way to the win the way a Water or Fire Rat might. It engineers the win, then executes without the wasted motion. When Metal's moral clarity holds the reins, this is the most formidable Rat there is ... principled, exacting, the strategist who plays hard but tells you the rules first. That version gets trusted the way you trust a blade you can see.\n\nThe shadow is the same blade with nobody watching it. Metal's discipline can harden past principle into ruthlessness, and the Rat's calculation, stripped of warmth, starts looking like exactly what it is. This is the Rat people watch most carefully, because the precision that builds an empire is the same precision that cuts people out of it without a flicker. Metal isolates ... it holds a standard so high and unbending that the Metal Rat can end up alone at the top of a perfect system, having quietly calculated everyone else out of it. The gift and the danger are the same edge. It only ever matters which way it is turned.`,
  'Water Rat': `Water moves as undercurrent, and it turns the Rat's gift for reading a room into something close to reading minds. The Water Rat perceives ... it feels the mood of a situation before anyone has spoken, senses where the real power actually sits, knows which way the current is moving and moves with it. Where other Rats hunt for opportunity, the Water Rat simply notices that the opportunity has arrived, usually before its owner does. At its best it reads as wisdom of the quietest kind ... the advisor in the corner who never seems to be working an angle and is, somehow, always exactly right.\n\nThat invisibility is also the catch. You can see a Fire Rat scheme. You never see a Water Rat do anything at all ... which is precisely why, the moment someone realizes the Water Rat has been three moves ahead the entire time, the trust curdles into a particular wariness. Nothing is harder to forgive than the calculation you never noticed. And Water carries its own undertow ... the element that flows around every obstacle can flow around action itself, seeing everything, understanding everything, and sometimes doing nothing, letting the current carry it past chances it read perfectly and declined to take. The depth is real. The only question Water ever leaves open is whether it will move.`,

  'Wood Ox': `Wood is the element of growth, and it gives the Ox the one thing the Ox usually lacks ... a direction to bend toward. The Ox is the zodiac's great builder, patient and tireless, but left alone it tends to build whatever is already in front of it. Wood hands it a vision. The Wood Ox still does the slow, honest, unglamorous work that every Ox does, but now the labor is aimed at something growing rather than just something solid. That is the version people trust without reservation ... the worker who not only shows up but knows what the thing is for.\n\nThe shadow is the quiet war between Wood's flexibility and the Ox's fixity. Wood wants to branch, to try, to adapt; the Ox wants to set its feet and finish. When the two cooperate, the Wood Ox grows something durable. When they don't, the Ox usually wins, and the vision gets buried under the refusal to change the plan. Watch for the Wood Ox that started out idealistic and slowly became immovable, still talking about the garden while pouring another foot of concrete. The growth was real. The question is whether it kept growing or just hardened in place.`,
  'Fire Ox': `Fire is passion, and on the Ox it becomes something rare and a little overwhelming ... conviction with the strength to outlast everyone. Most fire blazes and fades. The Fire Ox blazes and then keeps going, applying that heat to the long, grinding work that fire usually has no patience for. This is the Ox that works for a cause and will not be moved off it, the one whose passion you trust precisely because it has the endurance to back it up. Where a Fire Tiger inspires and a Fire Rat performs, the Fire Ox simply outlasts ... still burning long after the room has gone home.\n\nThe shadow is the immovable object set on fire. The Ox's stubbornness fused with Fire's intensity produces a creature that cannot quit, even when quitting is the only sane move. The Fire Ox digs in on the wrong hill and burns there, mistaking endurance for being right. The same heat that makes its commitment magnificent makes its grudges permanent. People trust the Fire Ox until the day they realize it has been carrying a fight for fifteen years that everyone else forgot ... and that it has no intention of ever putting it down.`,
  'Earth Ox': `Earth on the Ox is a doubling. The Ox is already the most grounded animal in the cycle, and Earth is the element of ground itself ... so the Earth Ox is bedrock on bedrock, the steadiest combination in all sixty cells. This is the person the whole structure quietly rests on. No drama, no flash, no sudden moves ... just the absolute reliability of someone who will be exactly where you left them, doing exactly what they said, for as long as it takes. Of all the Oxen, this is the one whose steadiness reads most purely as strength. You build on the Earth Ox the way you build on stone.\n\nThe shadow is what happens to stone over time. It does not move. Double Earth can set the Ox's natural fixity into something total, a refusal so complete it stops looking like patience and starts looking like a monument to a single idea. The Earth Ox keeps holding a position long after the ground around it has shifted, certain that not moving is the same as being right. The strength is genuine, and it is the foundation everyone leans on. The thing to watch is the day the foundation becomes a wall ... the day the most dependable creature in the zodiac simply will not, under any argument, change its mind.`,
  'Metal Ox': `Metal is discipline, and it takes the Ox's raw capacity for work and gives it a standard. The Metal Ox does not just labor; it labors to specification. The result is the craftsman, the Ox whose patience is wedded to precision, who builds the thing right because building it wrong is not a thing this Ox can do. There is honor in it ... Metal carries a code, and the Metal Ox keeps its word the way it keeps a level line, exactly and without fuss. You trust the Metal Ox completely, because its standards are visible and it holds itself to them harder than it holds anyone else.\n\nThe shadow is the standard turned into a weapon. Metal's hardness layered over the Ox's stubbornness can produce the taskmaster ... rigid, exacting, unforgiving of the human messiness that does not meet spec. The Metal Ox can become the person nobody can satisfy, the one whose principles have calcified into judgment. And Metal isolates: a standard held that high, that immovably, eventually leaves the Metal Ox alone with its perfect work and its disappointment in everyone who could not match it. The discipline built something real. The danger is the day it stops building and starts only inspecting.`,
  'Water Ox': `Water is the element of flow, and it is the gentlest thing that can happen to an Ox. The Ox's great limitation is that it does not bend ... Water teaches it how. The Water Ox keeps all the reliability and patience of its sign but gains something most Oxen never get: the ability to read the situation and adapt the approach without abandoning the work. It feels more, perceives more, and lets the current inform the labor. This is the Ox you trust not only to finish but to finish wisely, the steady hand that also happens to understand the room.\n\nThe shadow is the Ox that flows a little too easily. Water's passivity, settling into the Ox's reluctance to make sudden moves, can produce a deep inertia ... the Water Ox that sees everything clearly, agrees with everything reasonably, and slowly stops initiating anything at all. The strength quietly becomes drift. The same depth that makes this Ox perceptive can make it a follower of currents, content to be carried, mistaking going along for going somewhere. The reliability is still there. The question Water leaves on this Ox is whether it is still pulling the plow ... or just floating along beside it.`,

  'Wood Tiger': `Wood gives the Tiger a cause, and a Tiger with a cause is a formidable thing. On its own the Tiger is raw force ... courage looking for somewhere to point itself. Wood points it at growth, at other people, at something worth protecting, and turns the fighter into the champion. The Wood Tiger charges, but it charges for someone, and the generosity in it is real ... this is the Tiger that uses its considerable nerve to clear a path so others can walk through. People follow the Wood Tiger because its courage is in service, not on display.\n\nThe shadow is Wood's habit of reaching everywhere at once meeting the Tiger's habit of charging in. The Wood Tiger takes up too many causes, charges too many hills, spreads its formidable force across more crusades than any one creature can win. The idealism is genuine and the energy is enormous, but a Tiger scattered across nine fights protects nobody well. Watch the Wood Tiger that started as a champion and became a perpetual crusader, always charging, rarely finishing, exhausting the very people it meant to defend. The courage was never the problem. Knowing where to spend it was.`,
  'Fire Tiger': `Fire on the Tiger is a doubling, and it produces the most magnetic creature in the entire cycle. The Tiger is already fire ... add the element and you get pure charisma, the leader the room organizes itself around without being asked. The Fire Tiger is bold past the point most people can sustain, electric, impossible to look away from, and when that energy is aimed well it inspires whole rooms to be braver than they are. This is the Tiger as a natural force. You trust it the way you trust a bonfire ... gratefully, and from a sensible distance.\n\nThe shadow is also the doubling. Two measures of fire on the zodiac's most domineering animal is a great deal of heat with very little governor on it. The Fire Tiger can tip from inspiring into overpowering, leadership into domination, courage into the reckless need to win every exchange ... and Fire's tax always comes due, so it burns itself down in public, spectacularly, on schedule. The same flame that makes the Fire Tiger a magnet makes it exhausting to stand near for long. The room loves the blaze. The room also, quietly, learns to watch its own eyebrows.`,
  'Earth Tiger': `Earth is the element of patience, and patience is exactly what the Tiger never has. The Earth Tiger is the rare one that learned to wait. All the courage and competitive fire are still there, but Earth grounds the charge ... it picks its battles, paces its strength, and strikes when striking actually works rather than the instant the blood rises. This is the Tiger as strategist, the warrior whose nerve is matched by judgment. You trust the Earth Tiger because it is brave and it is not going to get you killed proving it.\n\nThe shadow is the Tiger's force fused with Earth's refusal to move. When it works, you get strategic patience. When it does not, you get a stubborn aggression that has dug in on a position and will defend it past all reason ... the Earth Tiger that decided it was right two years ago and has been immovably charging the same wall since. The grounding that should produce wisdom can instead produce a slow, heavy, unstoppable wrongness. The courage is real and the patience is real. The danger is the day they combine into a creature that cannot back down and will not hurry, planting its feet on the wrong ground and calling it strength.`,
  'Metal Tiger': `Metal sharpens the Tiger into the general. Where most Tigers are courage at a full sprint, the Metal Tiger is courage under command ... force disciplined into strategy, aggression organized into something exact and deliberate. It still hits hard, harder than most, but it hits where it decided to hit, by a code it actually keeps. When Metal's moral clarity holds the reins, this is the protector you want between you and the danger ... principled, controlled, unwilling to spend the considerable force it carries on anyone who has not earned it. You trust the Metal Tiger the way you trust a sword with a scabbard.\n\nThe shadow is the sword out of the scabbard with no one to answer to. Metal's hardness on the Tiger's domination can produce the conqueror ... cold, ruthless, certain, force without the warmth that would tell it when to stop. The Metal Tiger can become the one who wins every battle and notices, too late, the field it cleared to do it. And Metal isolates the way it always does: a will held that hard leaves the Metal Tiger commanding a territory nobody else wanted to live in. The discipline is what makes it formidable. The same discipline, gone cold, is what makes it feared.`,
  'Water Tiger': `Water deepens the Tiger and, strangely, makes it more dangerous by making it quieter. The Tiger's instinct is the open charge ... Water teaches it to read the room first. The Water Tiger still has all the courage, but it now perceives where the real power sits, senses the current, and moves with an intelligence the brawling Tigers never develop. This is the Tiger that wins before the fight looks like it started, the one whose nerve is wrapped in genuine intuition. You trust it because it is brave and it actually sees you ... it fights for people it has bothered to understand.\n\nThe shadow is Water's undertow on the Tiger's force. The same depth that makes the Water Tiger perceptive can make it indirect, and a Tiger that has learned not to charge openly can learn instead to undermine, to move in the current rather than meet things in the open. At its worst, Water's passivity drowns the Tiger's courage entirely ... the fighter that sees every battle clearly and quietly declines to enter any of them, calling avoidance wisdom. The perception is a gift. The question Water always leaves is whether the Tiger will use what it sees to act ... or just to explain, beautifully, why it did not.`,

  'Wood Rabbit': `Wood is growth, and on the Rabbit it turns grace into something that actually builds. The Rabbit is the diplomat of the cycle, gifted at smoothing and harmonizing, but grace alone can stay on the surface. Wood roots it ... the Wood Rabbit uses its social intelligence to grow real connection, to nurture people rather than just please them, to make the kind of peace that holds because something genuine is underneath it. This is the Rabbit whose kindness you trust because it keeps producing, season after season, the warmth that other Rabbits only perform.\n\nThe shadow is Wood's overreach meeting the Rabbit's deepest reflex, which is to avoid the hard moment. A garden needs pruning, and the Wood Rabbit hates to cut. So it grows in every direction, says yes to everyone, tends the healthy and the harmful with the same gentle hand, and avoids the one difficult conversation that would have saved the whole bed. The generosity is real. But kindness that will not ever wound, even to heal, eventually lets the weeds take the garden ... and the Wood Rabbit stands in the middle of it, having been lovely to absolutely everyone, wondering how it all got so tangled.`,
  'Fire Rabbit': `Fire warms the Rabbit, and the result is charm with actual heat in it. The cool, elegant Rabbit becomes magnetic, socially radiant, the one whose grace pulls people in rather than just putting them at ease. The Fire Rabbit can work a room like no other sign and mean it ... the warmth is genuine, the perceptiveness is sharp, and the combination is genuinely disarming. You trust the Fire Rabbit because around it you feel genuinely seen, and most of the time you are.\n\nThe shadow is what fire does to a creature that already prefers harmony to honesty. The Rabbit avoids conflict; Fire makes it good at avoiding it charmingly. The Fire Rabbit can turn the impulse to please into a performance so warm that no one notices it is also a way of never saying the true, difficult thing. And Fire burns out ... the Fire Rabbit pours itself into being liked until there is nothing left, then resents the very people it charmed for taking what it never learned to stop offering. The warmth is real and the charm is a gift. The thing to watch is the moment the radiance becomes a way to keep everyone at exactly the distance where they can be delighted but never let close.`,
  'Earth Rabbit': `Earth grounds the Rabbit, and grounding is what the Rabbit's grace needs most. On its own the Rabbit's diplomacy can float ... pleasant, perceptive, but light. Earth gives it weight and follow through. The Earth Rabbit builds harmony that lasts, the steady peacemaker who does not just soothe the moment but tends the relationship for years, dependable and calm and quietly essential. This is the Rabbit whose gentleness reads as real strength, because it is attached to something solid. You trust the Earth Rabbit to still be there, still kind, still keeping the peace, long after the charming ones have moved on.\n\nThe shadow is Earth's immovability settling into the Rabbit's avoidance. A Rabbit that hates conflict and an element that hates change can together produce a very stable false peace ... the Earth Rabbit that keeps everything smooth on the surface by never, ever addressing what is wrong underneath. The steadiness becomes entrenchment. The harmony becomes a wall that nothing difficult is allowed past. The Earth Rabbit can hold a comfortable arrangement together for decades through sheer refusal to disturb it, and call that loyalty. The peace is real. The question is whether it is peace ... or just a very well kept quiet over something that needed to be said out loud.`,
  'Metal Rabbit': `Metal gives the Rabbit a spine, and a Rabbit with a spine is a different creature entirely. The Rabbit's gift is grace; Metal's is the willingness to draw a line. Put them together and you get the diplomat who can actually say no ... refined, exacting, with taste sharpened to a standard and the backbone to hold it. The Metal Rabbit keeps all the social intelligence of its sign but loses the spinelessness that sometimes comes with it. You trust the Metal Rabbit because its kindness has edges ... it means what it says, and it will not flatter you into a wall.\n\nThe shadow is grace turned cold. Metal's hardness on the Rabbit's polish can produce an elegant cruelty ... the diplomat who has learned to cut with perfect manners, to wound precisely while remaining technically gracious. The Metal Rabbit can become the one whose disapproval is exquisite and merciless, whose refined standards are a way of holding everyone at a measured, judging distance. And Metal isolates: taste held that high, a line drawn that firmly, can leave the Metal Rabbit alone in a beautifully appointed room, having found everyone slightly wanting. The spine is what redeems the Rabbit. The same spine, gone cold, is what makes its politeness something to be careful around.`,
  'Water Rabbit': `Water on the Rabbit is a deepening of what the Rabbit already is. The Rabbit perceives; Water makes it nearly psychic. The Water Rabbit feels the emotional weather of a room before anyone speaks, senses what people need before they ask, and moves through social life with an attunement that can seem like a sixth sense. This is the gentlest, most empathic combination in the cycle ... grace married to genuine depth, the friend who always somehow knows. You trust the Water Rabbit because it understands you, often better than you understand yourself.\n\nThe shadow is two soft things compounding. The Rabbit avoids; Water flows around. Together they can produce a creature with almost no center of its own ... the Water Rabbit that feels everyone's feelings so completely it forgets to have its own, that dissolves into whatever room it is in, that avoids every conflict by simply flowing somewhere else. The perceptiveness becomes a way of disappearing. The empathy becomes an excuse for never taking a position. The Water Rabbit can spend a whole life beautifully attuned to everyone and never once say plainly what it wants. The depth is real and rare. The only question Water leaves it is whether there is anyone home behind all that lovely understanding.`,

  'Wood Dragon': `Wood gives the Dragon a reason to be enormous. The Dragon is the only mythical animal in the cycle, built on a scale the others are not ... vision, charisma, the natural assumption of greatness. Left alone, that scale serves itself. Wood points it outward, toward growth and other people, and turns the emperor into the benefactor. The Wood Dragon dreams big on someone else's behalf, builds things meant to outlast and include, spends its considerable gravity lifting what is around it. People rise for the Wood Dragon because its grandeur has somewhere generous to go.\n\nThe shadow is Wood's reach multiplying the Dragon's appetite for scale. The Wood Dragon's visions can grow faster than any ground can hold them ... a dozen magnificent plans, each one real in the telling, none of them quite touching the earth. The idealism becomes sprawl, the greatness becomes a series of beginnings, and the Dragon that meant to build a forest leaves behind a field of half dug foundations and a great deal of inspiring talk. The vision is genuine. The question is whether anything got finished underneath it.`,
  'Fire Dragon': `Fire on the Dragon is a doubling of everything already large, and it produces the most spectacular creature in the entire cycle. The Dragon's charisma plus Fire's heat is pure magnetism at imperial scale ... the leader a room does not so much follow as orbit. When the Fire Dragon aims that blaze well, it moves crowds, founds movements, makes the impossible look merely difficult. You do not forget meeting one. The confidence is total and, for long stretches, completely earned.\n\nThe shadow is also the doubling, and on a creature this size it is a great deal to manage. Two measures of fire under the Dragon's ego can tip vision into grandiosity, leadership into the need to be worshipped, confidence into an arrogance that no longer hears the word no. And Fire's tax comes due even here ... the Fire Dragon flames out enormously, in public, taking a fair amount of scenery with it. The same heat that made it a sun makes it impossible to be near once it decides the orbit is about it. People are inspired by the Fire Dragon. They are also, in time, a little scorched.`,
  'Earth Dragon': `Earth is the one element that can actually hold a Dragon. Most Dragons live slightly above the ground, in the realm of vision and ambition ... Earth brings one down to where things get built. The Earth Dragon is the rare practical visionary, the one whose greatness comes with a foundation under it, who dreams at full Dragon scale and then, unfashionably, does the patient work to make the dream real. This is the empire that lasts because someone laid the stone. You trust the Earth Dragon because its grandeur keeps its promises.\n\nThe shadow is what Earth does to anything over time, which is set it. The Dragon's confidence fused with Earth's immovability can produce a monarch who has decided how things are and will not be moved off it ... the grand vision calcified into doctrine, the empire defended long after the world it was built for has changed. The Earth Dragon can become a monument to its own younger greatness, magnificent and slightly fossilized. The achievement is real and built to last. The danger is the day it stops building and starts only guarding what it already made.`,
  'Metal Dragon': `Metal forges the Dragon into something formidable past the ordinary meaning of the word. The Dragon's natural authority, sharpened by Metal's discipline and precision, becomes the steel emperor ... exacting, principled, powerful in a way that does not bluster because it does not need to. When Metal's code holds the reins, this is greatness you can rely on absolutely. The Metal Dragon sets a standard at imperial scale and meets it first, and the people under it are protected by the same hardness that built the throne. You trust the Metal Dragon the way you trust something proven under load.\n\nThe shadow is power with the warmth stripped out. Metal's coldness on the Dragon's ego can produce the tyrant proper ... ruthless, absolute, certain, a greatness that has stopped asking whether it should and only asks whether it can. The Metal Dragon can win the whole board and rule it alone, having held a standard so unbending that no one was permitted close enough to stay. The discipline is what makes it magnificent. The same discipline, gone cold, is what makes its court so very quiet.`,
  'Water Dragon': `Water deepens the Dragon and trades some of its thunder for sight. Most Dragons impose their vision on the world ... the Water Dragon reads the world first, senses where history is actually moving, and aligns its considerable power with the current rather than against it. This is the wisest of the Dragons, greatness married to genuine perception, the leader who is large enough to command and deep enough to know when not to. You trust the Water Dragon because its ambition has eyes.\n\nThe shadow is Water's undertow working on a creature whose whole nature is to act big. The same depth that makes the Water Dragon perceptive can make it hesitant ... the grand vision endlessly refined and never quite launched, the enormous potential content to remain potential. Water can drown even a Dragon's fire, leaving a creature that sees the whole magnificent possibility and somehow, decade after decade, declines to build it. The depth is real and the vision is vast. The only question Water leaves a Dragon is whether all that seeing ever becomes doing.`,

  'Wood Snake': `Wood warms the Snake's cool intelligence and gives it something to grow. The Snake is the deep strategist of the cycle, patient and perceptive, but its wisdom can coil inward, kept for its own purposes. Wood draws it out ... the Wood Snake uses its formidable insight to mentor, to cultivate, to grow other people the way it grows its own understanding. This is the sage rather than the schemer, depth in the service of something living. People trust the Wood Snake because its intelligence is generous, and a generous strategist is a rare and valuable thing.\n\nThe shadow is Wood's tendency to take on more than it can tend meeting the Snake's habit of working several layers at once. The Wood Snake can have a dozen quiet projects growing in a dozen directions, each subtle, each carefully managed, until even the Snake loses track of which vine is which. The cultivation becomes a tangle. The patient depth becomes overcommitment hidden so well that no one, the Snake included, can quite see the whole of it. The wisdom is real. The risk is a garden so intricate it strangles itself.`,
  'Fire Snake': `Fire warms the Snake into something genuinely compelling. The cold, watchful Snake gains heat and charisma, and the combination is magnetic in a way the other Snakes are not ... deep intelligence wrapped in real warmth, insight that draws you in rather than keeping you at a careful distance. The Fire Snake is the strategist you actually want to be near, the one whose perceptiveness comes with charm enough to make the depth feel like a gift rather than a scan. You trust it because it is brilliant and it seems, convincingly, to be on your side.\n\nThe shadow is the most dangerous combination the Snake offers. Fire's charisma layered over the Snake's secrecy can produce the seducer in the old sense ... the one whose warmth is the cover for an agenda you do not see until it has already worked. The Fire Snake can charm and calculate in the same breath, and the heat makes the maneuver almost impossible to catch in the moment. And Fire burns its own schemes down eventually, often spectacularly. The brilliance is real. The thing to watch is whether the warmth is the gift or the bait.`,
  'Earth Snake': `Earth grounds the Snake into the steady sage. The Snake's wisdom, set on Earth's patient foundation, stops being elusive and becomes dependable ... the quiet advisor whose counsel is built slowly, tested against years, and almost always right. The Earth Snake does not dazzle. It accumulates, holding its perceptions until they harden into something solid enough to stand on, and the people who learn to listen to it come to rely on it completely. You trust the Earth Snake because its depth has roots and its judgment keeps.\n\nThe shadow is Earth's immovability settling into the Snake's suspicion and possessiveness. A creature that watches carefully and an element that does not let go can together produce a deep, patient grudge ... the Earth Snake that decided long ago not to trust someone and has quietly, immovably held that position ever since. The wisdom calcifies into wariness, the depth into a refusal to release. The Earth Snake can hold a suspicion the way it holds everything else, indefinitely, mistaking the grip for discernment. The insight is genuine. The danger is the day it stops perceiving and only guards.`,
  'Metal Snake': `Metal sharpens the Snake into a blade of pure strategy. The Snake's intelligence, disciplined by Metal's precision, becomes surgical ... it sees the whole board, finds the exact point of leverage, and acts on it with a cleanness that leaves no wasted motion. When Metal's code governs, this is the most formidable mind in the cycle, principled and exacting, the strategist who plays a long game by rules it actually keeps. You trust the Metal Snake the way you trust a scalpel ... precisely, and with respect for what it could do.\n\nThe shadow is the scalpel with no ethic behind it. Metal's coldness on the Snake's secret depths can produce the consummate manipulator ... calculation stripped of warmth, perception used purely as advantage, the long game played without a flicker of mercy for the pieces. The Metal Snake can engineer outcomes most people never even see being built, and feel nothing about the cost. And Metal isolates: a mind that exact, that hidden, ends up trusting no one and trusted by few. The precision is the gift. Turned cold, it is exactly the thing the Snake's old reputation warns about.`,
  'Water Snake': `Water on the Snake is a deepening of the deepest sign in the cycle, and it produces something close to oracle. The Snake already perceives more than it says ... Water takes that to its limit, an intuition so fluid it reads people before they have decided to reveal anything, a wisdom that seems to come from somewhere beneath thought. The Water Snake is the one who simply knows, who feels the truth of a situation in the body before the facts arrive. At its best it is profound, and the people who trust it trust it on a level they cannot quite explain.\n\nThe shadow is two depths compounding into the unreachable. The Snake withholds; Water dissolves the edges. Together they can produce a creature no one ever truly knows ... the Water Snake that perceives everything, reveals nothing, and slowly disappears into its own depths, holding every secret including, eventually, itself. Water's passivity can leave all that oracle wisdom doing nothing but observing. The depth is real and rare and a little frightening. The only question Water leaves the Snake is whether any of it ever surfaces, or whether it all just goes down.`,

  'Wood Horse': `Wood gives the Horse's gallop a direction worth running. The Horse is freedom and motion, energy that has to move, but motion without aim is just a lot of dust. Wood hands it a horizon ... the Wood Horse runs toward something, channels its considerable vitality into growth and adventure that actually builds, brings other people along on the ride. This is the free spirit as inspiration, the one whose momentum lifts a whole group because it is clearly headed somewhere good. You trust the Wood Horse because its freedom has a purpose you can see.\n\nThe shadow is Wood's tendency to branch everywhere meeting the Horse's tendency to bolt. The Wood Horse can take off in nine directions at once, each one promising, none of them ridden to the end ... the adventurer who starts every journey and finishes few, scattering its lovely energy across more roads than any one life can travel. The freedom becomes flightiness with a noble justification attached. The vitality is real. The question is whether it ever arrives anywhere, or just keeps discovering new places to start running.`,
  'Fire Horse': `Fire on the Horse is a doubling of an animal already made of motion, and tradition has never quite known what to do with it. The Fire Horse is the wildest cell in the cycle ... pure exhilarating energy, independence turned electric, a free spirit with the throttle welded open. In Chinese and Japanese folklore the Fire Horse year carries a fierce reputation, and birth rates have measurably dropped in some Fire Horse years out of old superstition about how intense its people run. Set the superstition aside and what remains is real enough: this is vitality at a voltage most lives are not wired for. Aimed, it is unforgettable.\n\nThe shadow is the throttle that does not close. Two units of fire on the Horse's restlessness produces a creature that genuinely cannot be reined ... that bolts from every fence, burns through every commitment, and runs itself into the ground because slowing down feels like dying. The same blaze that makes the Fire Horse magnificent makes it nearly impossible to keep. The freedom is total. So, often, is the cost of never once choosing to stand still.`,
  'Earth Horse': `Earth is the element the Horse least expects and most needs. The Horse runs; Earth teaches it that arriving is also a thing. The Earth Horse keeps all the vitality and independence of its sign but gains the rare capacity to stay ... to finish the journey, to build something where it landed, to turn adventure into a life rather than a string of departures. This is the free spirit that can also be relied upon, the one whose energy you trust because it has learned to point at the long haul. Of all the Horses, this is the one that gets somewhere and keeps it.\n\nThe shadow is the war between an animal that must move and an element that wants to root. When the two fight, the Earth Horse can stall ... caught between the need to run and the need to settle, it sometimes does neither, pawing the ground in place. The vitality goes nowhere, the steadiness never quite takes, and the Horse ends up restless and stuck at once, the worst of both. The grounding is a gift when it cooperates with the gallop. The danger is the day it becomes a fence the Horse built around itself.`,
  'Metal Horse': `Metal gives the Horse's wild energy a discipline, and a disciplined Horse is a champion. All the drive and independence remain, but Metal lays a track under them ... focus, training, a standard the Horse holds itself to. The Metal Horse does not just run; it competes, it masters, it turns raw vitality into genuine achievement through sheer applied will. This is the free spirit that became formidable, the one whose energy you trust because it has been forged into direction. When Metal's code holds, the Metal Horse is the athlete of the cycle in every sense.\n\nThe shadow is the track turned into a cage. Metal's rigidity on the Horse's drive can produce a creature that runs hard on a single fixed line and cannot conceive of another ... ambition narrowed to a groove, freedom sacrificed entirely to the goal, the independent spirit ironically imprisoned by its own discipline. And Metal isolates: a Horse running that hard, that exactly, often runs alone, leaving the herd behind as a distraction. The discipline is what makes it win. The danger is a creature that forgot freedom was the point and kept running anyway, long after the race stopped mattering.`,
  'Water Horse': `Water deepens the Horse and gives its motion some perception. The Horse runs on instinct ... Water adds intuition, a feel for the current, so the Water Horse moves not just fast but well, reading the terrain, flowing toward the opening rather than just charging the field. This is the adaptable adventurer, the free spirit with a sixth sense for where the good road actually is. You trust the Water Horse because its freedom is intelligent ... it goes where it goes for reasons it could not always name but that usually turn out right.\n\nThe shadow is Water's drift settling into the Horse's restlessness, and together they can produce pure aimlessness. The Water Horse can flow and run at the same time and still arrive nowhere ... carried by every current, chasing every interesting eddy, mistaking constant pleasant motion for a direction. Water's passivity hands the Horse a permission slip to never choose, and the vitality dissipates into a life of lovely, perpetual, unmoored wandering. The freedom is real and the perception is real. The only question Water leaves the Horse is whether it is swimming somewhere or just being swept.`,

  'Wood Goat': `Wood gives the Goat's gentleness something to grow. The Goat is the cycle's tender artist, empathetic and creative, but its softness can turn inward into worry. Wood turns it outward instead ... the Wood Goat nurtures, cultivates beauty, grows warmth in the people around it, using its sensitivity as a gift rather than a wound. This is the Goat whose kindness builds something ... the friend who makes everyone feel cared for, the artist whose work actually nourishes. You trust the Wood Goat because its softness is generous, pointed at the world instead of curled around its own anxiety.\n\nThe shadow is Wood's habit of spreading wide meeting the Goat's tendency to lean. The Wood Goat can grow attached in too many directions, nurturing everyone and depending on all of them at once, until the generosity and the neediness become hard to tell apart. The care given quietly turns into care required, and the Goat that gave so freely finds it cannot stand without the very people it was tending. The warmth is real. The question is whether it stays a gift or slowly becomes a debt the Goat needs everyone else to keep paying.`,
  'Fire Goat': `Fire warms the Goat into something expressive and bright. The gentle, often quiet Goat gains heat, charisma, a flair for the dramatic ... the Fire Goat is the artist who performs, the tender heart with stage presence, warmth turned outward into something people gather around. The empathy is still there, sharpened now into the ability to move a room emotionally, and the creativity runs hot and vivid. You trust the Fire Goat because its feeling is real and it is not afraid to show it.\n\nThe shadow is what fire does to a creature governed by worry. The Goat's anxieties, set alight, become dramatic ... the Fire Goat can tip into emotional theater, neediness turned into a performance, the small fear inflated into a crisis that pulls everyone in. And Fire burns out, so the Fire Goat pours feeling until it is empty and then needs the room to refill it. The warmth and the drama come from the same place. The thing to watch is the day the genuine emotion becomes a way of being managed and reassured, the tender artist quietly running on everyone else's attention.`,
  'Earth Goat': `Earth grounds the Goat, and grounding is exactly what the worried, drifting Goat needs. The Goat's gifts are real but they can float ... Earth gives them a place to stand. The Earth Goat builds a stable, beautiful life, turns its creativity into something dependable, offers a gentle warmth that does not waver because it has solid ground under it. This is the artist who also pays the rent, the tender soul who is somehow also the most reliable person in the room. You trust the Earth Goat because its softness comes with roots.\n\nThe shadow is Earth's love of comfort meeting the Goat's dependence, which together produce a deep reluctance to risk. The Earth Goat can settle so thoroughly into the safe and the comfortable that it never ventures, never grows, never tests the gift ... clinging to the secure arrangement, leaning on the steady relationship, mistaking comfort for contentment. The grounding that should free the creativity instead pens it in. The stability is genuine and rare. The danger is the day the safe harbor becomes the whole world, and the Goat forgets it ever wanted to make anything new.`,
  'Metal Goat': `Metal gives the Goat a backbone its sign usually lacks. The Goat is soft, yielding, easily swayed ... Metal hardens it into something with standards and resolve. The Metal Goat keeps its creativity and empathy but gains a spine, a refined and exacting taste, the ability to hold a line and finish what it starts. This is the artist with discipline, the gentle one who turns out to be tougher than anyone expected. You trust the Metal Goat because its softness has structure ... it feels deeply and it will not be pushed around.\n\nThe shadow is Metal's hardness pressing on the Goat's sensitivity, which is a difficult fit. The exacting standard turned on a tender heart can become a way of punishing itself ... the Metal Goat that holds itself to an impossible mark and bleeds quietly when it misses, the artist whose perfectionism wars with its own feeling. It can turn brittle, the way soft things do when forced to be rigid, and the criticism it spares others it spends entirely on itself. The structure is what redeems the Goat. The danger is the day the standard stops protecting the gift and starts slowly crushing it.`,
  'Water Goat': `Water on the Goat is a deepening of the most feeling sign in the cycle. The Goat already runs on empathy ... Water takes it to its limit, an emotional attunement so complete that the Water Goat seems to feel what a room feels, to absorb other people's states as its own, to create from a place beneath words. This is the purest empath in the matrix, the artist whose work comes straight from the deep water, the friend who understands without being told. At its best it is profoundly tender and quietly wise.\n\nThe shadow is two soft things with nothing to hold the edges. The Goat dissolves easily; Water dissolves everything. Together they can produce a creature with no boundary at all ... the Water Goat overwhelmed by feeling that is not even its own, swept by every current, unable to tell where it ends and the room begins. Water's passivity leaves it adrift, the empathy curdling into a helplessness that needs constant rescue. The depth of feeling is the gift. The danger is the day it has no shore, and the tender, perceptive Goat simply goes under, taking all that beautiful sensitivity with it.`,

  'Wood Monkey': `Wood gives the Monkey's cleverness something to build. The Monkey is the cycle's inventor, quick and versatile and endlessly entertaining, but its genius can spend itself on pure play. Wood roots it ... the Wood Monkey aims its inventiveness at growth, at problems worth solving, at making things that help and last. This is the clever mind in constructive mode, the innovator rather than the trickster, wit put to work. You trust the Wood Monkey because its brilliance has somewhere to go besides its own amusement.\n\nThe shadow is Wood's sprawl meeting the Monkey's restlessness, a combination practically designed to start everything and finish nothing. The Wood Monkey can have ten inventions going at once, each genuinely clever, all of them abandoned the moment the next interesting thing appears. The versatility becomes scatter. The genius becomes a graveyard of half built brilliant ideas. The cleverness is real and the energy is delightful. The question is whether any of it gets carried far enough to matter, or just keeps producing dazzling beginnings the Monkey is too curious to ever complete.`,
  'Fire Monkey': `Fire on the Monkey is the entertainer turned star. The Monkey's wit, set alight, becomes dazzling ... charisma, comic timing, an inventive brilliance that does not just solve the problem but makes the solving a performance. The Fire Monkey is the one who lights up the room and runs it, quick and warm and impossible to look away from. The cleverness is genuine and the charm is real, and when it is pointed well, the Fire Monkey creates and entertains at a level the cooler Monkeys never reach. You trust it because the brilliance is generous, handed to the room as delight.\n\nThe shadow is the trickster with a spotlight. Fire's charisma on the Monkey's mischief can produce the charming manipulator ... the one whose cleverness works people rather than problems, whose performance is a way of never being pinned down or held to account. And Fire burns through its material, so the Fire Monkey can leave a trail of dazzled, slightly used people behind it. The wit is the gift. The thing to watch is whether it is in service of something, or just in service of the Monkey staying the most interesting thing in the room.`,
  'Earth Monkey': `Earth grounds the Monkey, and a grounded Monkey is a genuinely useful thing. The Monkey's cleverness, set on Earth's practical foundation, stops being mere cleverness and becomes problem solving ... invention aimed at real situations, wit applied to things that actually need fixing. The Earth Monkey is the one whose ideas work, the inventor who also builds the thing, versatility with follow through. You trust the Earth Monkey because its brilliance is practical and it does not leave you holding a clever mess.\n\nThe shadow is the friction between an animal that needs novelty and an element that wants to stay put. The Earth Monkey can chafe against its own grounding ... the restless, inventive mind pressing against the patient, rooted element, producing a low constant frustration, a cleverness that feels caged by the practical. At its worst the Monkey's mischief turns sly and small, the bored genius working angles out of restlessness rather than need. The grounding is what makes the Monkey effective. The danger is the day the steady ground starts to feel like a trap, and all that inventiveness has nowhere good to go.`,
  'Metal Monkey': `Metal sharpens the Monkey into a precision instrument. The Monkey's quick inventiveness, disciplined by Metal, becomes formidable problem solving ... not just clever but exact, the mind that finds the elegant solution and executes it cleanly. The Metal Monkey is the engineer of the cycle, wit organized into mastery, versatility focused to a point. When Metal's code holds, this is genius with integrity, brilliance you can build on. You trust the Metal Monkey because its cleverness is disciplined and it means what it makes.\n\nThe shadow is cleverness with the warmth taken out. Metal's hardness on the Monkey's trickster streak can produce something cold ... the manipulator who calculates rather than plays, who uses that quick brilliant mind to outmaneuver people precisely and without much feeling about it. The Metal Monkey can win every exchange and reduce every interaction to a problem to be solved, including the human ones. And Metal isolates, so the cleverest creature in the room ends up a little alone with its perfect solutions. The precision is the gift. Turned cold, the wit becomes a weapon the Monkey forgets to put down.`,
  'Water Monkey': `Water deepens the Monkey and gives its wit some soul. The Monkey is clever; Water makes it perceptive, adding an intuitive read of people to the inventive mind, so the Water Monkey is not just quick but genuinely understanding ... socially brilliant, emotionally attuned, the entertainer who actually knows what the room needs. This is the most likable Monkey, cleverness softened into charm that connects rather than just impresses. You trust the Water Monkey because its brilliance comes with empathy ... it sees you, and it uses that to delight rather than to use you.\n\nThe shadow is Water's slipperiness on the Monkey's mischief, which can produce the hardest creature in the cycle to ever pin down. The Water Monkey can read exactly what you want and become it, flow around every attempt to hold it accountable, use its perception to manipulate so smoothly you thank it afterward. Water's passivity can also leave the brilliance unused, the clever mind drifting on its own perceptiveness, entertaining itself and going nowhere. The depth is real and the wit is real. The only question Water leaves the Monkey is whether all that seeing serves connection ... or just a very pleasant way of never being caught.`,

  'Wood Rooster': `Wood gives the Rooster's sharp eye something to grow rather than just inspect. The Rooster is the cycle's perfectionist, observant and exacting and honest to a fault, but that precision can curdle into criticism. Wood turns it constructive ... the Wood Rooster uses its standards to build people up, to improve things into being, to point out the flaw and then help fix it. This is the exacting eye in service of growth, the honest critic who actually wants you to get better. You trust the Wood Rooster because its standards are generous ... it holds you to them because it believes you can meet them.\n\nThe shadow is Wood's habit of reaching everywhere at once meeting the Rooster's compulsion to notice every flaw. The Wood Rooster can find something to improve in absolutely everything, scattering its sharp attention across every detail of every situation until the helpfulness becomes a constant, exhausting hum of correction. The standards multiply faster than anyone can meet them. The honest improving eye becomes a person who cannot let a single thing simply be fine. The precision is real and well meant. The question is whether it ever rests, or just keeps finding the next thing that is almost right.`,
  'Fire Rooster': `Fire on the Rooster is the performer in full plumage. The Rooster already loves to be seen doing the thing well ... Fire turns that into genuine showmanship, confidence, a charismatic command of the stage. The Fire Rooster does excellent work and makes sure the room knows it, and most of the time the work earns the spotlight it demands. There is real heat and real skill here, standards held high and delivered with flair. You trust the Fire Rooster because the confidence is backed by the goods.\n\nThe shadow is what fire does to the Rooster's vanity. The pride in the work can tip into pride in the appearance of the work ... the Fire Rooster preening, performing competence, more invested in being seen as excellent than in quietly being it. And the criticism runs hot: a Fire Rooster displeased is blunt and burning, the honest streak turned into public scorching. Fire burns out, so the relentless need to shine and be admired eventually exhausts everyone, the Rooster included. The skill is real. The thing to watch is the day the strut gets ahead of the substance, and the crowing starts arriving before the work is actually done.`,
  'Earth Rooster': `Earth grounds the Rooster into the methodical craftsman, and this is the combination tradition knows best ... the 1969 Earth Rooster, precision and watchfulness laid on patient ground. The Earth Rooster's exacting eye is matched to real follow through; it does not just notice what is wrong, it does the slow, careful work of making it right, and keeps doing it long after flashier types have lost interest. This is dependable excellence, standards you can build a life on. You trust the Earth Rooster because its precision shows up every single day and finishes what it starts.\n\nThe shadow is Earth's immovability hardening the Rooster's criticism into something fixed. The Earth Rooster can settle into its judgments the way it settles into everything ... the standard becomes dogma, the honest assessment becomes a permanent quiet disapproval that nothing and no one is ever quite exempt from. The methodical eye stops adjusting and just grinds, finding the same faults in the same way for years. The reliability is genuine and the standards are real. The danger is the day the careful craftsman becomes the immovable critic, certain it is right because it has been finding the same problems for so long.`,
  'Metal Rooster': `Metal on the Rooster is precision squared. The Rooster is already the most exacting animal in the cycle, and Metal is the element of the exact ... so the Metal Rooster is the perfectionist's perfectionist, the standard against which other standards get measured. Its eye misses nothing, its work is impeccable, its honesty is total and unflinching. When that precision is aimed at a craft, the Metal Rooster produces something close to flawless, and you trust it completely to get the details exactly, unimpeachably right.\n\nThe shadow is the most merciless critic the matrix can produce. Doubled precision with Metal's coldness turns the Rooster's honest eye into a blade ... fault found everywhere, stated bluntly, with no cushion of warmth to soften the cut. The Metal Rooster can be impossible to satisfy, including and especially for itself, holding a standard so absolute that reality is permanently disappointing. And Metal isolates, so the most exacting creature in the cycle often ends up alone with its perfect, unshared standards. The precision is genuinely extraordinary. The danger is a creature that sees every flaw so clearly it can no longer see anything that is simply, humanly good enough.`,
  'Water Rooster': `Water softens the Rooster's hard edges and gives its sharp eye some mercy. The Rooster observes and judges ... Water adds perception and feeling, so the Water Rooster sees just as clearly but understands what it sees, reading the why behind the flaw rather than just flagging the flaw. This is the discerning one whose standards come with empathy, the honest eye that delivers the hard truth in a way you can actually hear. You trust the Water Rooster because its precision is kind ... it notices everything and chooses, wisely, what to say.\n\nThe shadow is Water's passivity dampening the Rooster's drive, and an anxious perceptiveness taking the place of action. The Water Rooster can see every flaw and every danger with perfect clarity and then simply worry about them, the sharp eye turned into a source of unending quiet anxiety rather than useful correction. It perceives the problem, feels the problem, and does nothing about the problem, drifting on its own watchfulness. The discernment is the gift. The danger is the day all that clear seeing becomes only fretting, the Rooster who notices everything wrong and lacks the will to set any of it right.`,

  'Wood Dog': `Wood gives the Dog's loyalty room to grow and a generous shape. The Dog is the cycle's faithful protector, principled and honest and devoted, but its sense of duty can narrow into worry. Wood opens it ... the Wood Dog extends its loyalty outward, fights for fairness on a wide scale, protects not just its own but whoever needs protecting. This is devotion as a generous force, integrity that builds and includes, the friend who has your back and somehow everyone else's too. You trust the Wood Dog completely, because its loyalty is real and it is pointed at something larger than itself.\n\nThe shadow is Wood's reach meeting the Dog's deep capacity for worry. The Wood Dog can take on the burdens of everyone in range, carrying every cause and every friend's trouble as its own, until the generous loyalty becomes a crushing, anxious overcommitment. It cannot say no to anyone who needs it, and it needs everyone to be alright before it can rest, which means it never quite rests. The devotion is genuine and it is a gift. The question is whether the Dog is protecting the people it loves or slowly drowning under the weight of everyone it could not bring itself to turn away.`,
  'Fire Dog': `Fire on the Dog is the defender ablaze. The Dog's loyalty and sense of justice, set alight, become a cause ... the Fire Dog fights for what is right with real passion and real courage, the warm fierce protector who will put itself between you and the danger without a second thought. There is heat and heart here in equal measure, devotion you can feel and integrity that does not flinch. You trust the Fire Dog because its loyalty is fierce and its principles are not for sale.\n\nThe shadow is the principle turned into a pyre. Fire on the Dog's judgment can produce the zealot ... the righteous crusader who has decided what is right and will burn anyone who disagrees, the protector whose fierce loyalty curdles into an us and them that leaves no room for nuance. The honest streak becomes harsh, the justice becomes wrath, and Fire's tax means the Dog exhausts itself defending a hill it may have chosen in haste. The courage is real and the heart is real. The thing to watch is the day the defender becomes the inquisitor, certain of its righteousness, burning in the name of a good it has stopped examining.`,
  'Earth Dog': `Earth grounds the Dog into the steadfast one, and steadfastness is the Dog at its best. The Dog's loyalty, set on Earth's patient foundation, becomes utterly unshakeable ... the friend who is simply always there, the principle that does not bend, the protector you can build a life around because it will not move and will not quit. The Earth Dog is dependability itself, devotion proven over decades, integrity you never have to wonder about. You trust the Earth Dog the way you trust the ground.\n\nThe shadow is Earth's immovability hardening the Dog's principles into rigidity. The Earth Dog can set its moral positions in stone the way it sets everything ... right and wrong fixed forever, judgment that cannot be revisited, a stubbornness about what is proper that leaves no room for the messy, changing truth of actual people. It can hold a grievance or a verdict for life, certain that not changing its mind is the same as integrity. The loyalty is genuine and the principles are real. The danger is the day the steadfast protector becomes the immovable judge, unable to forgive because forgiving would mean the ground had shifted.`,
  'Metal Dog': `Metal sharpens the Dog into the incorruptible guardian. The Dog's honesty and principle, disciplined by Metal, become a code held with absolute exactness ... the Metal Dog does not just believe in justice, it embodies it, unbribable, unwavering, the one whose integrity is a kind of architecture. When that hardness is in service of what is right, this is the most trustworthy creature in the cycle, the protector whose word is iron and whose loyalty cannot be bought or bent. You trust the Metal Dog the way you trust a law that actually holds.\n\nThe shadow is justice with the mercy filed off. Metal's coldness on the Dog's judgment can produce the merciless enforcer ... principle so rigid it forgets the people it was meant to protect, righteousness so exact it becomes cruel, the guardian who applies the code without a flicker of human give. The Metal Dog can be right and unbearable at once. And Metal isolates, so the most principled creature in the cycle often ends up alone, having held a standard no actual person could live up to, including the ones it loved. The integrity is genuine and rare. The danger is the day the code matters more than the people it was written for.`,
  'Water Dog': `Water deepens the Dog and gives its loyalty understanding. The Dog is faithful and principled ... Water adds perception and empathy, so the Water Dog protects with insight, judges with compassion, reads the people it is devoted to instead of just defending them on principle. This is loyalty tempered by wisdom, the protector who sees the whole person and stays anyway, justice that bends, mercifully, to fit the actual situation. You trust the Water Dog because its devotion comes with eyes ... it knows you, flaws and all, and is loyal to the real you rather than an idea of you.\n\nThe shadow is Water's undertow on the Dog's worry, which together can produce a creature that drowns in its own anxious perception. The Water Dog feels every danger to the people it loves, perceives every threat and every disappointment, and can sink under the weight of it ... loyalty turned into chronic dread, protection turned into a worry that helps no one. Water's passivity can leave it paralyzed, seeing all that needs defending and unable to act. The depth and the devotion are real. The only question Water leaves the Dog is whether it can love that completely without being swallowed by the fear of loss that comes with it.`,

  'Wood Pig': `Wood gives the Pig's generosity room to grow into something abundant. The Pig is the cycle's open heart, sincere and kind and genuinely pleased by the good things in life, and Wood turns that warmth into a force for growth ... the Wood Pig gives freely, nurtures lavishly, makes the people around it more comfortable and more cared for simply by being there. This is generosity as a kind of fertility, the host who makes everyone feel welcome, the friend whose kindness is bottomless and sincere. You trust the Wood Pig because its warmth asks for nothing back.\n\nThe shadow is Wood's tendency to overgrow meeting the Pig's tendency to overindulge. The Wood Pig can give past all sense, say yes to every pleasure and every request, expand its generosity and its appetites until both are out of any reasonable bound ... the host who overdoes it, the giver who depletes itself, the open heart that never learned where to stop. The kindness is real and the warmth is genuine. The question is whether the generosity stays nourishing or tips into excess, the Wood Pig giving and indulging until there is nothing left for it to give or enjoy.`,
  'Fire Pig': `Fire warms the Pig into pure celebration. The Pig already loves the good things ... Fire turns that into warmth you can feel across a room, a passionate, generous, joyful heart that throws itself into pleasure and into people with total sincerity. The Fire Pig is the life of the gathering, the one whose enthusiasm for living is contagious, whose generosity comes with real heat behind it. There is nothing calculated here ... the Fire Pig means every bit of its warmth, and you trust it because its joy and its kindness are obviously, disarmingly real.\n\nThe shadow is what fire does to a creature already inclined toward indulgence. The Pig's appetites, set alight, become excess ... the Fire Pig overdoing every pleasure, burning hot through food and drink and feeling and money, mistaking more for better until the celebration tips into something it cannot afford. And Fire burns out, so the joyful heart can crash hard when the party ends. The warmth and the joy are genuine gifts. The thing to watch is the day the love of life becomes the inability to say enough, the Fire Pig consuming the very pleasures it loves until they stop loving it back.`,
  'Earth Pig': `Earth grounds the Pig into the contented provider. The Pig's generosity and warmth, set on Earth's solid foundation, become dependable abundance ... the Earth Pig builds a comfortable life and shares it freely, the steady, sincere presence who makes a real home and welcomes everyone into it. There is nothing flighty here; the kindness is reliable, the generosity sustainable, the pleasures earned and enjoyed without apology. You trust the Earth Pig because its warmth has roots and its door is always, genuinely open.\n\nThe shadow is Earth's love of comfort compounding the Pig's love of pleasure, which together can produce a deep complacency. The Earth Pig can settle so contentedly into its comfortable life that it stops growing, stops striving, stops wanting anything beyond the pleasant arrangement it has built ... abundance softening into indulgence, contentment into inertia, the good life becoming a very comfortable rut. The stability is genuine and the warmth is real. The danger is the day comfort becomes the only value, and the generous, contented Pig quietly trades every ambition it ever had for one more easy, pleasant afternoon.`,
  'Metal Pig': `Metal gives the Pig a backbone, and a Pig with a backbone is sturdier than anyone expects. The Pig is soft, generous, easily imposed upon ... Metal hardens it into something with resolve and principle. The Metal Pig keeps all its sincerity and warmth but gains the spine to protect them, the determination to follow through, a quiet toughness underneath the kindness. This is the generous one you cannot take advantage of, the open heart with a firm hand, sincerity backed by genuine strength. You trust the Metal Pig because its kindness is a choice, not a weakness ... it gives because it wants to, and it knows exactly where its line is.\n\nThe shadow is Metal's hardness setting the Pig's appetites and its trust into something stubborn. The Metal Pig can dig in on its pleasures and its principles with a surprising obstinacy ... the gentle creature turned unexpectedly immovable, gripping what it wants or what it believes with a stubbornness that does not match its easy surface. Or the famous Pig trust, hardened rather than outgrown, becomes a refusal to see what it would rather not. The strength is what makes this Pig formidable. The danger is the day the backbone becomes mere stubbornness, and the open heart closes around a position it will no longer reconsider.`,
  'Water Pig': `Water deepens the Pig and gives its generosity real emotional reach. The Pig is kind ... Water makes it empathetic, adding a depth of feeling to the open heart, so the Water Pig does not just give freely but feels with people, senses what they need, offers a warmth that comes from genuine understanding rather than easy goodwill. This is the most emotionally generous cell in the cycle, sincerity married to depth, the friend whose kindness reaches all the way down. You trust the Water Pig because its warmth is not just pleasant ... it is felt, and it sees you.\n\nThe shadow is two yielding things compounding into the boundless. The Pig gives; Water flows around every obstacle, including its own limits. Together they can produce a creature that pours itself out completely ... the Water Pig feeling everyone's needs so deeply it cannot stop giving, absorbing other people's troubles until it is drained, mistaking total availability for love. Water's passivity leaves it unable to refuse, carried along on other people's currents, generous to the point of disappearing. The depth of feeling and the generosity are real and rare. The only question Water leaves the Pig is whether it can keep a little of itself, or whether it gives until there is no one left to do the giving.`,
}

// Lookup for a combined animal x element interpretation, falling back to the
// generic element description for cells not yet written in ANIMAL_ELEMENT.
export function getAnimalElement(animal: string, element: string): string {
  return ANIMAL_ELEMENT[`${element} ${animal}`] ?? ELEMENT_DESCRIPTIONS[element]
}

